pyra-backlight (0.8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * pyra-idle-dim: while mate-screensaver's unlock dialog is up, the
    keyboard light and the screen are back at their levels, so the
    password is not typed in the dark. They go down again if the dialog
    closes without an unlock. The screensaver sends no signal for the
    dialog, so this checks for its child process every 0.5 s while the
    screensaver is active.

 -- pyra-backlight maintainers <pyra-backlight@example.invalid>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0000

pyra-backlight (0.7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * pyra-idle-dim: on reload, a keyboard level changed to a number is set
    at once (or at wake, if the light is off for idle). Until now a level
    saved in the settings app took effect only at the next login.
  * pyra-greeter-kbd-light: reads its level from kbd-brightness-on-ac in
    60_pyra-backlight.gschema.override (20 if unreadable), so the two can
    no longer disagree.

 -- pyra-backlight maintainers <pyra-backlight@example.invalid>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000

pyra-backlight (0.6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Schema override setting org.mate.power-manager kbd-brightness-on-ac to
    20, the login screen's level. mate-power-manager sets the keyboard light
    to that key when it starts on AC; at its default of 100 the light jumped
    to full for about a second at each login before pyra-idle-dim set the
    user's level.

 -- pyra-backlight maintainers <pyra-backlight@example.invalid>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 20:00:00 +0000

pyra-backlight (0.5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * pyra-greeter-kbd-light: lightdm greeter-setup-script that sets the
    keyboard light to 20% before each login screen, at boot and after every
    logout. Until now the login screen kept whatever level the session had
    at logout, which could be off.
  * pyra-idle-dim.service: ConditionUser=!@system, so it no longer runs in
    the greeter's session as the lightdm user, where it set the keyboard
    light to lightdm's own recorded level (often 0) 30 s into the greeter.

 -- pyra-backlight maintainers <pyra-backlight@example.invalid>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

pyra-backlight (0.4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * pyra-idle-dim: read the screen dim depth from
    ~/.config/pyra-backlight.conf ([screen] dim = 1-100 or off), reread on
    SIGHUP. "off" turns screen dimming off and keeps the keyboard light
    handling.
  * pyra-idle-dim: never dim the screen below 1, the lowest lit step.
  * pyra-idle-dim.service: no longer sets PYRA_DIM_PERCENT. The variable
    is still read when the file has no [screen] dim key.

 -- pyra-backlight maintainers <pyra-backlight@example.invalid>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

pyra-backlight (0.3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * pyra-idle-dim: turn the keyboard light (*::kbd_backlight LED) off when
    the session goes idle and back on when it wakes, unless it was changed
    meanwhile.
  * pyra-idle-dim: set the keyboard light's level at login, after
    mate-power-manager's reset, from ~/.config/pyra-backlight.conf
    ([keyboard] level = last or 0-100). "last" is kept in
    ~/.local/state/pyra-backlight/kbd-level.
  * pyra-idle-dim.service: ExecReload rereads the config file (SIGHUP).
  * Restart pyra-idle-dim at each MATE login from /etc/xdg/autostart. The
    user unit alone started only with the systemd user manager, so after a
    logout and login with the manager still up (an ssh session open) idle
    dimming was not running.
  * postinst: reload running systemd user managers, which otherwise keep
    the old user unit until reboot.

 -- pyra-backlight maintainers <pyra-backlight@example.invalid>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

pyra-backlight (0.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Applet: drive the keyboard light as an LED (*::kbd_backlight) when the
    kernel declares it that way, in the same nine steps as the stock
    kernel's backlight. The stock kernel's path still works.

 -- pyra-backlight maintainers <pyra-backlight@example.invalid>  Sat, 26 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0000

